    "id": "notion-microbiology-0003",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 3,
    "stem": "Which antibody titers indicate recent or recurrent streptococcal infection and are found MOST OFTEN in rheumatic individuals?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Anti-Streptolysin O"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Anti-hyaluronidase"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Anti-DNase B"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Anti-M protein"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Streptolysin O is an immunogenic, beta-hemolysin. Antibody to Streptolysin O is a marker of recent mucosal infections used in the diagnosis of rheumatic heart disease (ASO titer) along with clinical symptoms. An elevated titer is also proof of a previous streptococcal infection. It is usually more elevated after a pharyngeal than skin infection, while the ADB is typically elevated regardless of the site of the infection.\nM proteins are anchored in Strep. pyogenes’ cytoplasmic membrane with Class I M proteins extending through the cell wall to the cell surface although Class II M proteins do not. Strains causing rheumatic heart disease have exposed Class I M proteins, which are responsible for cross-reactivity with human heart antigens.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Streptolysin O is an immunogenic, beta-hemolysin. Antibody to Streptolysin O is a marker of recent mucosal infections used in the diagnosis of rheumatic heart disease (ASO titer) along with clinical symptoms. An elevated titer is also proof of a previous streptococcal infection. It is usually more elevated after a pharyngeal than skin infection, while the ADB is typically elevated regardless of the site of the infection.\nM proteins are anchored in Strep. pyogenes’ cytoplasmic membrane with Class I M proteins extending through the cell wall to the cell surface although Class II M proteins do not. Strains causing rheumatic heart disease have exposed Class I M proteins, which are responsible for cross-reactivity with human heart antigens.",
      "B": "Anti-hyaluronidase antibodies are not typically used as markers for streptococcal infections or rheumatic disease. Hyaluronidase is an enzyme produced by certain bacteria, including streptococci, to facilitate the spread of infection through tissues. However, anti-hyaluronidase antibodies are not as commonly measured in clinical practice as ASO or anti-DNase B antibodies.",
      "C": "Anti-DNase B antibodies are another type of antibody produced in response to streptococcal infection. Like ASO titers, elevated levels of anti-DNase B antibodies indicate exposure to streptococcal antigens and are used as a marker for recent or recurrent streptococcal infections. These antibodies are also associated with rheumatic fever.",
      "D": "M proteins are surface proteins of Streptococcus pyogenes. While M proteins play a role in the pathogenesis of streptococcal infections, including rheumatic fever, measuring anti-M protein antibodies is not a common practice for routine diagnosis or monitoring. ASO and anti-DNase B antibodies are more commonly used in this context."
    },
