    "id": "notion-microbiology-0005",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 5,
    "stem": "Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding poststreptococcal diseases?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The rates of nephritis and rheumatic fever are similar following untreated related infection"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Rheumatic fever has a marked tendency to be reactivated by recurrent streptococcal infection"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Either skin infection or pharyngitis precedes rheumatic fever"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Antistreptococcal chemoprophylaxis is recommended in glomerulonephritis patients"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Someone who had rheumatic fever in the past is more likely to get rheumatic fever again if they get strep throat or scarlet fever again. Based on currently available evidence, ARF is exclusively caused by infection of the upper respiratory tract with group A streptococci. Although classically, certain M-serotypes (particularly types 1, 3, 5, 6, 14, 18, 19, 24, 27, and 29) were associated with ARF, in high-incidence regions, it is now thought that any strain of group A streptococcus has the potential to cause ARF. The potential role of skin infection and of groups C and G streptococci is currently being investigated.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement is not accurate. The rates of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN) and rheumatic fever can vary following untreated streptococcal infections. While both conditions can arise from untreated streptococcal infections, their rates are not necessarily similar. The development of acute poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis is more commonly associated with certain strains of group A streptococci, particularly those causing skin infections, while rheumatic fever is often linked to pharyngitis caused by group A streptococci.",
      "B": "Someone who had rheumatic fever in the past is more likely to get rheumatic fever again if they get strep throat or scarlet fever again. Based on currently available evidence, ARF is exclusively caused by infection of the upper respiratory tract with group A streptococci. Although classically, certain M-serotypes (particularly types 1, 3, 5, 6, 14, 18, 19, 24, 27, and 29) were associated with ARF, in high-incidence regions, it is now thought that any strain of group A streptococcus has the potential to cause ARF. The potential role of skin infection and of groups C and G streptococci is currently being investigated.",
      "C": "This statement is not entirely accurate. While rheumatic fever can be preceded by either skin infections or pharyngitis caused by group A streptococci, pharyngitis is more commonly associated with the development of rheumatic fever. Group A streptococcal pharyngitis is a well-known precursor to rheumatic fever, especially in high-incidence regions.",
      "D": "Antistreptococcal chemoprophylaxis is not routinely recommended in patients with glomerulonephritis. It is, however, recommended for individuals with a history of rheumatic fever to prevent recurrent episodes. The goal of prophylaxis is to prevent streptococcal infections that could trigger a recurrence of rheumatic fever."
    },
