    "id": "notion-microbiology-0010",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 10,
    "stem": "What diagnostic procedure can be done which will be strongly suggestive of endocarditis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Electrocardiogram"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Chest X-ray"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Echocardiogram"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "(+) Blood culture"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Infective endocarditis is life-threatening; identification of the underlying etiology informs optimized individual patient management. Changing epidemiology, advances in blood culture techniques, and new diagnostics guide the application of laboratory testing for diagnosis of endocarditis. Blood cultures remain the standard test for microbial diagnosis, with directed serological testing (i.e., Q fever serology, Bartonella serology) in culture-negative cases.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "An electrocardiogram is a valuable diagnostic tool, but it is not considered strongly suggestive of endocarditis. ECG is useful for assessing the electrical activity of the heart and identifying abnormalities such as arrhythmias or conduction disturbances. However, the definitive diagnosis of endocarditis typically requires other diagnostic procedures.",
      "B": "A chest X-ray is not considered a specific diagnostic test for endocarditis. While it may reveal certain complications or findings associated with endocarditis, such as congestive heart failure or pulmonary infiltrates, it is not a primary tool for confirming the diagnosis of endocarditis.",
      "C": "An echocardiogram, particularly a transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE), is strongly suggestive of endocarditis. Echocardiography allows visualization of heart valves, detection of vegetations, assessment of valvular function, and identification of complications such as abscess formation or valve perforation. TEE is more sensitive than transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) for detecting valvular lesions and is a key diagnostic procedure for endocarditis.",
      "D": "Infective endocarditis is life-threatening; identification of the underlying etiology informs optimized individual patient management. Changing epidemiology, advances in blood culture techniques, and new diagnostics guide the application of laboratory testing for diagnosis of endocarditis. Blood cultures remain the standard test for microbial diagnosis, with directed serological testing (i.e., Q fever serology, Bartonella serology) in culture-negative cases."
    },
