    "id": "notion-microbiology-0014",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 14,
    "stem": "Which of the following will least likely differentiate Taenia saginata from Taenia solium?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Presence of hooks"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Number of uterine branches"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Appearance of scolex"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Gross morphology of eggs"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Gross morphology of eggs is not a differentiating factor between Taenia saginata and Taenia solium. The eggs of these two species are morphologically indistinguishable. Microscopic examination, as well as consideration of other features like the number of uterine branches and appearance of the scolex, is necessary for accurate identification.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The presence of hooks is a distinguishing factor between T. solium and T. saginata. The scolex of T. solium has 4 suckers with a double row of hooks, while T. saginata has 4 suckers without hooks.",
      "B": "The number of uterine branches is a reliable characteristic for differentiating Taenia saginata from Taenia solium. T. saginata typically has a greater number of uterine branches (14-35) compared to T. solium, which usually has fewer branches (7-20). This difference is a key feature in the morphological identification of these tapeworms.",
      "C": "The appearance of the scolex is a distinguishing feature between Taenia saginata and Taenia solium. The scolex is the anterior end of the tapeworm, and in T. saginata, it is characterized by four strong suckers and lacks hooks. In contrast, T. solium has a scolex with four suckers and a circular double-row of hooks.",
      "D": "Gross morphology of eggs is not a differentiating factor between Taenia saginata and Taenia solium. The eggs of these two species are morphologically indistinguishable. Microscopic examination, as well as consideration of other features like the number of uterine branches and appearance of the scolex, is necessary for accurate identification."
    },
