    "id": "notion-microbiology-0023",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 23,
    "stem": "Situation 3 - A 28 year old full term pregnant delivered her baby while she is having at the same time painful grouped vesicles located on the left mucosal side of her vaginal wall.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Neonatal Herpes"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Localized zoster"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Neonatal varicella"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Herpetic whitlow"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Genital herpes is characterized by vesiculoulcerative lesions of the penis of the male or of the cervix, vulva, vagina, and perineum of the female. The lesions are very painful and may be associated with fever, malaise, dysuria, and inguinal lymphadenopathy.\nHSV infection of the newborn may be acquired in utero, during birth, or after birth. The mother is the most common source of infection in all cases. Neonatal herpes is estimated to occur in about 1 in 5000 deliveries per year. The newborn infant seems to be unable to limit the replication and spread of HSV and has a propensity to develop severe disease.\nThe most common route of infection (~75% of cases) is for HSV to be transmitted to a newborn during birth by contact with herpetic lesions in the birth canal.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Genital herpes is characterized by vesiculoulcerative lesions of the penis of the male or of the cervix, vulva, vagina, and perineum of the female. The lesions are very painful and may be associated with fever, malaise, dysuria, and inguinal lymphadenopathy.\nHSV infection of the newborn may be acquired in utero, during birth, or after birth. The mother is the most common source of infection in all cases. Neonatal herpes is estimated to occur in about 1 in 5000 deliveries per year. The newborn infant seems to be unable to limit the replication and spread of HSV and has a propensity to develop severe disease.\nThe most common route of infection (~75% of cases) is for HSV to be transmitted to a newborn during birth by contact with herpetic lesions in the birth canal.",
      "B": "Localized zoster is caused by the reactivation of the varicella-zoster virus, typically manifesting as a painful rash with grouped vesicles along a dermatomal distribution. It is not directly related to the scenario described during childbirth.",
      "C": "Neonatal varicella refers to varicella-zoster virus infection in a newborn. It is usually associated with maternal chickenpox during pregnancy rather than genital lesions during childbirth. The scenario described is more indicative of herpes simplex virus rather than varicella-zoster virus.",
      "D": "Herpetic whitlow is a localized infection of the fingers or hands caused by herpes simplex virus. It is not directly related to the scenario of a pregnant woman delivering a baby with vesicles on the vaginal wall."
    },
