    "id": "notion-microbiology-0024",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 24,
    "stem": "The possible cause of death of the newborn with disseminated viral disease of this etiology, is which of the following?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bacterial infection"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Encephalitis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Intravascular coagulopathy"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cardiac septal defect"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The overall mortality rate of untreated disease is 50%. Babies with neonatal herpes exhibit three categories of disease: (1) lesions localized to the skin, eye, and mouth; (2) encephalitis with or without localized skin involvement; and (3) disseminated disease involving multiple organs, including the central nervous system. The worst prognosis (~80% mortality rate) applies to infants with disseminated infection, many of whom develop encephalitis.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "While bacterial infection can be a cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality, in the context of disseminated viral disease, the primary concern is the viral infection itself. Neonatal herpes, especially in the disseminated form, has a significant impact on mortality.",
      "B": "The overall mortality rate of untreated disease is 50%. Babies with neonatal herpes exhibit three categories of disease: (1) lesions localized to the skin, eye, and mouth; (2) encephalitis with or without localized skin involvement; and (3) disseminated disease involving multiple organs, including the central nervous system. The worst prognosis (~80% mortality rate) applies to infants with disseminated infection, many of whom develop encephalitis.",
      "C": "Intravascular coagulopathy refers to abnormal blood clotting throughout the blood vessels, and it can be a complication of severe infections, including disseminated viral diseases. However, in the context of neonatal herpes, the primary cause of mortality is often related to the viral infection itself, including encephalitis.",
      "D": "Cardiac septal defects are congenital heart abnormalities and are not directly related to disseminated viral disease. In the context of neonatal herpes, mortality is more likely attributed to complications such as encephalitis or disseminated organ involvement."
    },
