    "id": "notion-microbiology-0025",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 25,
    "stem": "What will be the sequelae of the neonate who had survived this severe infection?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Cardiomyopathy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Growth retardation"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Epidermal tissue abnormalities"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Neurologic impairment"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Neonatal herpes infections are almost always symptomatic. The overall mortality rate of untreated disease is 50%. Babies with neonatal herpes exhibit three categories of disease: (1) lesions localized to the skin, eye, and mouth; (2) encephalitis with or without localized skin involvement; and (3) disseminated disease involving multiple organs, including the central nervous system. The worst prognosis (∼80% mortality rate) applies to infants with disseminated infection, many of whom develop encephalitis. The cause of death of babies with disseminated disease is usually viral pneumonitis or intravascular coagulopathy. Many survivors of severe infections are left with permanent neurologic impairment.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Neonatal herpes primarily affects the skin, eyes, mouth, and central nervous system. While disseminated disease can involve multiple organs, including the heart, cardiomyopathy is not a common sequelae of neonatal herpes.",
      "B": "Growth retardation is not a typical sequelae of neonatal herpes. The long-term consequences often revolve around neurological impairment rather than growth-related issues.",
      "C": "Neonatal herpes can cause skin lesions as part of its clinical presentation. While acute skin manifestations may occur, it is less likely to result in long-term epidermal tissue abnormalities. The more significant concern is neurological sequelae.",
      "D": "Neonatal herpes infections are almost always symptomatic. The overall mortality rate of untreated disease is 50%. Babies with neonatal herpes exhibit three categories of disease: (1) lesions localized to the skin, eye, and mouth; (2) encephalitis with or without localized skin involvement; and (3) disseminated disease involving multiple organs, including the central nervous system. The worst prognosis (∼80% mortality rate) applies to infants with disseminated infection, many of whom develop encephalitis. The cause of death of babies with disseminated disease is usually viral pneumonitis or intravascular coagulopathy. Many survivors of severe infections are left with permanent neurologic impairment."
    },
