    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "This statement is true. Antibiotic susceptibility testing helps identify the most effective antibiotics for treating bacterial endocarditis. Since different bacteria may exhibit varying levels of susceptibility to antibiotics, performing susceptibility tests allows healthcare providers to select the most appropriate antibiotic regimen tailored to the specific infecting organism.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement is false. Acute/subacute bacterial endocarditis and rheumatic fever have distinct pathogeneses. Acute/subacute bacterial endocarditis is primarily caused by bacterial infection of the heart valves, often by organisms from the normal microbiota, while rheumatic fever is an inflammatory condition that can result from inadequately treated streptococcal throat infections, particularly those caused by Streptococcus pyogenes.",
      "B": "This statement is false. Prompt treatment with antibiotics is crucial in bacterial endocarditis, but it may not necessarily \"abort\" its development. The damage to heart valves and other complications may already be in progress. Early and appropriate antibiotic therapy is vital for controlling the infection and preventing further complications.",
      "C": "This statement is true. Antibiotic susceptibility testing helps identify the most effective antibiotics for treating bacterial endocarditis. Since different bacteria may exhibit varying levels of susceptibility to antibiotics, performing susceptibility tests allows healthcare providers to select the most appropriate antibiotic regimen tailored to the specific infecting organism.",
      "D": "This statement is false. The more common causative agents of subacute bacterial endocarditis are often members of the normal microbiota, such as viridans streptococci and enterococci. These bacteria can enter the bloodstream from the respiratory or gastrointestinal tract and cause infection in individuals with predisposing conditions, such as abnormal heart valves."
    },
