    "id": "notion-microbiology-0031",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 31,
    "stem": "Intracellular inhibitory proteins referred to as restriction factors have been identified. Which of the following is included among them?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "APOBEC3G"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Interferon"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "TM and env protein"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Tumor suppression gene"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Cells contain intracellular antiviral inhibitory proteins referred to as restriction factors. One type is APOBEC3G, a cytidine deaminase that inhibits HIV replication. The Vif protein promotes viral infectivity by suppressing the effects of APOBEC3G. Another inhibitory protein is TRIM5α, which binds to incoming retrovirus particles and recruits them to proteasomes before much viral DNA synthesis occurs.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Cells contain intracellular antiviral inhibitory proteins referred to as restriction factors. One type is APOBEC3G, a cytidine deaminase that inhibits HIV replication. The Vif protein promotes viral infectivity by suppressing the effects of APOBEC3G. Another inhibitory protein is TRIM5α, which binds to incoming retrovirus particles and recruits them to proteasomes before much viral DNA synthesis occurs.",
      "B": "Interferons are signaling proteins that play a crucial role in the antiviral response. They induce an antiviral state in cells, inhibiting viral replication and spread. While not a restriction factor per se, interferons contribute to the host's defense against viral infections.",
      "C": "TM (transmembrane) and env (envelope) proteins are components of the HIV viral structure. They are not typically referred to as restriction factors. Instead, they are involved in viral entry and fusion with host cells.",
      "D": "The term \"tumor suppression gene\" is broad and not specific to restriction factors. Tumor suppressor genes are involved in regulating cell growth and preventing the development of cancer. They are not directly related to the intracellular inhibition of viral replication."
    },
