    "id": "notion-microbiology-0034",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 34,
    "stem": "How does this organism elude the immune system of its host?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Quorum sensing"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Rapid switching of antigenic form"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Intracellular location"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Protective cell wall"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Antigen heterogeneity is a major consideration when studying humoral immunity in gonococcal infection. Pili, protein II (PII), and lipooligosaccharide (LOS) are the most important antigens, quantitatively, in generating antibody responses in gonococcal infection. These three antigens shift from one antigenic form to another at a frequency of 1 in 10^3 or greater. Because of the rapid shifting from one antigenic form to another, nearly every strain of N. gonorrhoeae may be antigenically distinct.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Quorum sensing is a mechanism used by bacteria to communicate with each other and coordinate gene expression based on population density. Neisseria gonorrhoeae does not primarily employ quorum sensing as a major immune evasion strategy.",
      "B": "Antigen heterogeneity is a major consideration when studying humoral immunity in gonococcal infection. Pili, protein II (PII), and lipooligosaccharide (LOS) are the most important antigens, quantitatively, in generating antibody responses in gonococcal infection. These three antigens shift from one antigenic form to another at a frequency of 1 in 10^3 or greater. Because of the rapid shifting from one antigenic form to another, nearly every strain of N. gonorrhoeae may be antigenically distinct.",
      "C": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae is an extracellular pathogen and primarily infects mucosal surfaces. While it can invade and survive within host cells, its main evasion strategy is not related to intracellular location. Instead, it often avoids the immune response through surface antigenic variation.",
      "D": "The cell wall of Neisseria gonorrhoeae does not provide a protective shield against the immune system. In fact, the bacterium is susceptible to host immune mechanisms, and its ability to switch surface antigens contributes more significantly to immune evasion."
    },
