    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "In the past, impetigo was either caused by either S. aureus or Group A Beta-hemolytic streptococci. Currently, however, the most frequently isolated pathogen in cases of impetigo is S. aureus.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Demodex mites are microscopic arachnids that inhabit hair follicles and sebaceous glands in humans. They are generally considered a normal part of the skin flora and are not known to cause impetigo. Their involvement is more related to certain skin conditions, such as demodex folliculitis, which is different from impetigo.",
      "B": "In the past, impetigo was either caused by either S. aureus or Group A Beta-hemolytic streptococci. Currently, however, the most frequently isolated pathogen in cases of impetigo is S. aureus.",
      "C": "Dermatophytes are fungi that cause infections of the skin, hair, and nails. They are commonly associated with conditions like ringworm. In the context of impetigo, bacterial pathogens, particularly Staphylococcus aureus, are more relevant.",
      "D": "Group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) can also cause impetigo, especially in combination with Staphylococcus aureus. Both bacteria can contribute to the characteristic symptoms of impetigo."
    },
