    "id": "notion-microbiology-0041",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 41,
    "stem": "Situation 9 – A 28 yo female consulted at the clinic because of sudden onset 3-4 days ago of dysuria, mucopurulent discharge, and frequent urination. Microscopic examination of the discharge did not show any fixed or motile organism. Gram-stained smear of the urethral discharge failed to show any intracellular diplococcic.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Candida albicans"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Trichomonas vaginalis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Herpes simplex virus"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Chlamydia trachomatis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Chlamydia trachomatis is a bacterium responsible for chlamydia, one of the most common sexually transmitted infections worldwide. Chlamydia infection can manifest with symptoms such as dysuria, mucopurulent discharge, and frequent urination, which align with the patient's presentation. However, Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium and may not always be visible on Gram stain or microscopic examination of the discharge.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Candida albicans is a fungus commonly associated with vaginal yeast infections, but it typically presents with symptoms such as vaginal itching, soreness, and a thick, white, cottage cheese-like discharge. While Candida albicans can cause vaginal infections, it is not the most probable causative organism based on the symptoms described, especially since there is no mention of vaginal itching or cottage cheese-like discharge.",
      "B": "Trichomonas vaginalis is a protozoan parasite responsible for trichomoniasis, a sexually transmitted infection (STI). Trichomoniasis typically presents with symptoms such as vaginal itching, burning, and a frothy, yellow-green, malodorous discharge. The absence of motile organisms on microscopic examination of the discharge makes Trichomonas vaginalis less likely as the causative organism.",
      "C": "Herpes simplex virus (HSV) can cause genital herpes, which often presents with painful genital ulcers, itching, and discomfort. However, mucopurulent discharge and dysuria are not typical symptoms of genital herpes. Additionally, the absence of intracellular diplococci on Gram stain makes HSV less likely as the causative organism.",
      "D": "Chlamydia trachomatis is a bacterium responsible for chlamydia, one of the most common sexually transmitted infections worldwide. Chlamydia infection can manifest with symptoms such as dysuria, mucopurulent discharge, and frequent urination, which align with the patient's presentation. However, Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium and may not always be visible on Gram stain or microscopic examination of the discharge."
    },
