    "id": "notion-microbiology-0042",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 42,
    "stem": "What is the test of choice to establish its diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Serology"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Nucleic acid amplification test"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Hybridization assay"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Culture"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) methodology consists of amplifying C. trachomatis DNA or RNA sequences using polymerase chain reaction (PCR), transcription-mediated amplification (TMA), or strand displacement amplification (SDA). These sensitive, specific tests have become the \"gold standard\" and are the preferred diagnostic method, if available.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Serology is not the test of choice for the diagnosis of current Chlamydia trachomatis infection. It is more useful for detecting past exposure or chronic infections. Testing for antibodies may not differentiate between current and past infections.",
      "B": "Nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) methodology consists of amplifying C. trachomatis DNA or RNA sequences using polymerase chain reaction (PCR), transcription-mediated amplification (TMA), or strand displacement amplification (SDA). These sensitive, specific tests have become the \"gold standard\" and are the preferred diagnostic method, if available.",
      "C": "Hybridization assays involve the detection of specific DNA or RNA sequences. While they can be used, NAATs are generally more sensitive and specific, making them the preferred choice.",
      "D": "Culture was historically used for Chlamydia diagnosis, but it is less sensitive than NAATs and may lead to false-negative results. Culture is not commonly recommended for routine diagnostic purposes."
    },
