    "id": "notion-microbiology-0050",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 50,
    "stem": "Situation 12 - A 35-year-old female consulted the clinic because of foul-smelling vaginal discharge. The discharge was whitish-gray in color, adherent to the vaginal wall, but none of the cervical os. A wet mount of the vaginal fluid was done. It showed no PMNs, (+) for clue cells with adherent bacilli. Discharge had a fishy odor. What is the MOST PLAUSIBLE clinical diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bacterial vaginosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Trichomonas vaginalis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "White ovulation - normal discharge"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Candida vulvovaginitis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Bacterial vaginosis is the most prevalent cause of symptomatic vaginitis. The vaginal discharge associated with bacterial vaginosis is thin and gray-white. Speculum examination reveals that the discharge is mildly adherent to the vaginal walls, in contrast to a physiologic discharge, which is in the most dependent areas of the vagina. In women with bacterial vaginosis, the most frequent symptom is an unpleasant vaginal odor, which patients describe as musty or fishy.\nThe four criteria for the diagnosis of bacterial vaginosis are:\nA homogeneous vaginal discharge is present.\nThe vaginal discharge has a pH of 4.5 or higher.\nThe vaginal discharge has an amine-like odor when mixed with potassium hydroxide, the whiff test.\nA wet smear of the vaginal discharge demonstrates clue cells more than 20% of the number of the vaginal epithelial cells.\nThe classic discharge of Trichomonas infection is termed frothy (with bubbles) and often has an unpleasant odor.\nFungal vaginitis usually presents as a vulvovaginitis. Pruritus is the predominant symptom. The vaginal discharge is white or whitish gray, highly viscous, and described as granular or floccular, with no odor.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Bacterial vaginosis is the most prevalent cause of symptomatic vaginitis. The vaginal discharge associated with bacterial vaginosis is thin and gray-white. Speculum examination reveals that the discharge is mildly adherent to the vaginal walls, in contrast to a physiologic discharge, which is in the most dependent areas of the vagina. In women with bacterial vaginosis, the most frequent symptom is an unpleasant vaginal odor, which patients describe as musty or fishy.\nThe four criteria for the diagnosis of bacterial vaginosis are:\nA homogeneous vaginal discharge is present.\nThe vaginal discharge has a pH of 4.5 or higher.\nThe vaginal discharge has an amine-like odor when mixed with potassium hydroxide, the whiff test.\nA wet smear of the vaginal discharge demonstrates clue cells more than 20% of the number of the vaginal epithelial cells.\nThe classic discharge of Trichomonas infection is termed frothy (with bubbles) and often has an unpleasant odor.\nFungal vaginitis usually presents as a vulvovaginitis. Pruritus is the predominant symptom. The vaginal discharge is white or whitish gray, highly viscous, and described as granular or floccular, with no odor.",
      "B": "The classic discharge in Trichomonas infection is frothy and may have an unpleasant odor. While Trichomonas infection can cause a fishy odor, the wet smear in this situation showed clue cells, which is more indicative of BV.",
      "C": "Normal ovulatory discharge tends to be clear or white and is not typically associated with a foul odor. In the given scenario, the presence of a fishy odor and clue cells points more towards BV rather than normal ovulation discharge.",
      "D": "Candida infections usually present with a white, curd-like discharge and are often associated with intense pruritus (itching). In this case, the clinical presentation and wet smear findings are more consistent with BV."
    },
