    "id": "notion-microbiology-0053",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 53,
    "stem": "Situation 13 - In a squatter colony, there were sporadic reports of cases of watery diarrhea which mostly lasts for 5-7 days without treatment. Sometimes the report reaches epidemic proportion but the local health officials did not pay serious attention because of the self-limited course of the diarrhea. One day however, a patient suffered from a more severe form of diarrhea which lasted for a month. It was later learned that the patient is suffering from AIDS.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Cryptosporidium hominis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Entamoeba histolytica"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Enterococci"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Clostridium perfringens"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Cryptosporidium species, typically Cryptosporidium hominis, can infect the intestine in immunocompromised persons (eg, those with AIDS) and cause severe, intractable diarrhea. They have long been known as parasites of rodents, fowl, rhesus monkeys, cattle, and other herbivores and have probably been an unrecognized cause of self-limited, mild gastroenteritis and diarrhea in humans. The prominent clinical feature of cryptosporidiosis is watery diarrhea, which is mild and self-limited (1–2 weeks) in normal persons but may be severe and prolonged in immunocompromised or very young or old individuals.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Cryptosporidium species, typically Cryptosporidium hominis, can infect the intestine in immunocompromised persons (eg, those with AIDS) and cause severe, intractable diarrhea. They have long been known as parasites of rodents, fowl, rhesus monkeys, cattle, and other herbivores and have probably been an unrecognized cause of self-limited, mild gastroenteritis and diarrhea in humans. The prominent clinical feature of cryptosporidiosis is watery diarrhea, which is mild and self-limited (1–2 weeks) in normal persons but may be severe and prolonged in immunocompromised or very young or old individuals.",
      "B": "Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite that can cause amoebic dysentery, a form of dysentery characterized by bloody and mucoid diarrhea. While Entamoeba histolytica is a potential cause of prolonged diarrhea, its prevalence and impact on immunocompromised individuals, such as those with AIDS, are not as significant as Cryptosporidium in this context.",
      "C": "Enterococci are bacteria that are part of the normal flora of the human intestines. They are not typically associated with causing prolonged watery diarrhea. Enterococci are more commonly implicated in urinary tract infections, endocarditis, and other infections.",
      "D": "Clostridium perfringens is a bacterium associated with food poisoning. It is not typically a cause of prolonged watery diarrhea, especially in the context of an immunocompromised individual with AIDS. Clostridium perfringens food poisoning is more commonly characterized by abdominal cramps and diarrhea, with a rapid onset."
    },
