    "id": "notion-microbiology-0056",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 56,
    "stem": "Situation 14 - A 55-year old fish vendor who is a diabetic, accidentally pricks his right hand with the shell of an oyster. The following day, the skin surrounding the pinprick became intensely painful, swollen, red and the center apparently filled with pus. In the evening, he was rushed to the hospital because his entire right hand and forearm have become very swollen and with areas of bullous eruptions. He was so highly febrile.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Vibrio vulnificus"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Clostridium perfringens"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Staphylococcus epidermidis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Streptococcus pyogenes"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "V. vulnificus is particularly apt to be found in oysters, especially in warm months. Bacteremia with no focus of infection occurs in persons who have eaten infected oysters and who have alcoholism or liver disease. Infection often proceeds rapidly, with development of severe disease. About 50% of the patients with bacteremia die. Wound infections may be mild but often proceed rapidly (over a few hours), with development of bullous skin lesions, cellulitis, and myositis with necrosis.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "V. vulnificus is particularly apt to be found in oysters, especially in warm months. Bacteremia with no focus of infection occurs in persons who have eaten infected oysters and who have alcoholism or liver disease. Infection often proceeds rapidly, with development of severe disease. About 50% of the patients with bacteremia die. Wound infections may be mild but often proceed rapidly (over a few hours), with development of bullous skin lesions, cellulitis, and myositis with necrosis.",
      "B": "Clostridium perfringens is a bacterium associated with gas gangrene. While it can cause soft tissue infections, the rapid progression and bullous eruptions described in the scenario are more characteristic of Vibrio vulnificus.",
      "C": "Staphylococcus epidermidis is a common skin commensal and is usually not associated with severe skin and soft tissue infections. It is more commonly implicated in infections related to medical devices.",
      "D": "Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Streptococcus) can cause skin and soft tissue infections, including cellulitis and erysipelas. However, the scenario's rapid progression, bullous eruptions, and association with seafood point more strongly to Vibrio vulnificus."
    },
