    "id": "notion-microbiology-0059",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 59,
    "stem": "Situation 15 - A 53-year old man suffers from the effects of childhood disease when he was 3, characterized by high grade fever which subsided but recurred after 2-3 days, and accompanied by muscle pains during the febrile period. Since then, he had patchy areas of flaccid paralysis and muscle atrophy.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Mycobacterium leprae"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Poliovirus"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Clostridium botulinum"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Herpes simplex"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "When an individual susceptible to infection is exposed to the virus, the response ranges from inapparent infection without symptoms to a mild febrile illness to severe and permanent paralysis. Most infections are subclinical; only about 1% of infections result in clinical illness.\nThe incubation period is usually 7–14 days, but it may range from 3 to 35 days.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Mycobacterium leprae is the causative agent of leprosy (Hansen's disease). Leprosy primarily affects the skin, peripheral nerves, mucosa of the upper respiratory tract, and eyes. It does not typically cause recurrent high-grade fevers with muscle pains and flaccid paralysis.",
      "B": "When an individual susceptible to infection is exposed to the virus, the response ranges from inapparent infection without symptoms to a mild febrile illness to severe and permanent paralysis. Most infections are subclinical; only about 1% of infections result in clinical illness.\nThe incubation period is usually 7–14 days, but it may range from 3 to 35 days.",
      "C": "Clostridium botulinum produces botulinum toxin, which causes botulism. Botulism typically presents with symmetric descending flaccid paralysis, but the recurrent febrile episodes described in the scenario are not characteristic of botulism.",
      "D": "Herpes simplex virus can cause various clinical manifestations, including oral and genital lesions, but it is not known to cause the recurrent febrile episodes and muscle paralysis described in the scenario."
    },
