    "id": "notion-microbiology-0075",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 75,
    "stem": "Which of the following pathogenic features explains the neurologic manifestation of the patient?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bacterial invasion of the CNS and local release of endotoxin"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Meningeal irritations from bacterial invasions"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Secondary bacterial attack on the peripheral nerves"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Distant effect of the enterotoxin acting as a neurotoxin"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Seizures are the most common neurologic manifestation, followed by lethargy and disorientation or coma, which often are referred to as encephalopathy. The pathophysiology of Shigella encephalopathy has not been elucidated. It is postulated that shiga toxin production is not essential for this lethal complication. Shiga toxin, first described in 1903, only produced by S. dysenteriae type 1, however, S. flexneri, and S. sonnei do not produce shiga toxin as demonstrated by toxin neutralization and DNA hybridization studies. Often encephalopathy results in the early development of brain edema. During the acute stage of encephalopathy serum and cerebrospinal fluid concentrations of inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6) become elevated. Complementary studies in a murine model also documented similar findings suggesting that host inflammatory response has a major role in the pathogenesis.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Shigella dysenteriae does not typically invade the central nervous system (CNS) to cause encephalopathy through local release of endotoxin. The neurologic manifestations are likely associated with other factors.",
      "B": "While Shigella dysenteriae can cause systemic symptoms and invade the colonic mucosa, it does not primarily cause meningeal irritation. The neurological symptoms observed are more likely related to systemic effects.",
      "C": "Shigella dysenteriae does not typically result in a secondary bacterial attack on peripheral nerves. The neurological symptoms are more directly related to the systemic effects of the infection.",
      "D": "Seizures are the most common neurologic manifestation, followed by lethargy and disorientation or coma, which often are referred to as encephalopathy. The pathophysiology of Shigella encephalopathy has not been elucidated. It is postulated that shiga toxin production is not essential for this lethal complication. Shiga toxin, first described in 1903, only produced by S. dysenteriae type 1, however, S. flexneri, and S. sonnei do not produce shiga toxin as demonstrated by toxin neutralization and DNA hybridization studies. Often encephalopathy results in the early development of brain edema. During the acute stage of encephalopathy serum and cerebrospinal fluid concentrations of inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6) become elevated. Complementary studies in a murine model also documented similar findings suggesting that host inflammatory response has a major role in the pathogenesis."
    },
