    "id": "notion-microbiology-0089",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 89,
    "stem": "Situation 25 - Mang Juan is a horticulturist. He consulted the clinic because multiple subcutaneous nodules and abscesses occurring along the lymphatics of his right upper extremity. He recalled a history of trauma incurred while tending his plants several months ago. The initial working impression of this case is to get biopsy materials or exudates from an ulcerated lesion and request for which of the following?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Tuberculosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Chromoblastomycosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Sporotrichosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Erythema nodosum"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Sporotrichosis (\"rose gardener's disease\") is caused by the dimorphic fungus Sporothrix schenckii. S. schenckii is found in or on plant materials such as rose, plum trees, or sphagnum moss. It is traumatically introduced by florist's wire, splinters, or rose or plum tree thorns into subcutaneous tissues.\nThis subcutaneous, nodular fungal disease is generally not painful. When it spreads via the lymphatics, it produces a chain of lesions on the extremities, with the older lesions ulcerating and the newer ones starting nodular.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Tuberculosis is primarily caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and its presentation typically involves pulmonary symptoms. While extrapulmonary forms exist, the scenario described by Mang Juan, with subcutaneous nodules and abscesses along the lymphatics, does not align with tuberculosis. Tuberculosis is not a fungal infection, and its diagnosis would involve microbiological and imaging studies relevant to TB.",
      "B": "Chromoblastomycosis is a chronic fungal infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissues caused by various dematiaceous (dark-walled) fungi. It usually presents with verrucous, nodular, or tumorous lesions. However, the scenario described, including the history of trauma and occurrence along the lymphatics, is more indicative of sporotrichosis.",
      "C": "Sporotrichosis (\"rose gardener's disease\") is caused by the dimorphic fungus Sporothrix schenckii. S. schenckii is found in or on plant materials such as rose, plum trees, or sphagnum moss. It is traumatically introduced by florist's wire, splinters, or rose or plum tree thorns into subcutaneous tissues.\nThis subcutaneous, nodular fungal disease is generally not painful. When it spreads via the lymphatics, it produces a chain of lesions on the extremities, with the older lesions ulcerating and the newer ones starting nodular.",
      "D": "Erythema nodosum is a skin condition characterized by painful, red nodules, usually on the shins. It is not caused by fungal infections and is not associated with the traumatic history described by Mang Juan."
    },
