    "id": "notion-microbiology-0092",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 92,
    "stem": "Situation 26 - in the medieval age, a deadly epidemic killed hundreds of thousands to millions of the population of Western Europe. The disease was characterized by either high-grade fever and painful lymphadenopathy or primarily pneumonia with or without meningitis, and terminating in disseminated intravascular coagulation and death.",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bartonella bacilliformis"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Legionella pneumophilia"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Yersinia pestis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Pasteurella multocida"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Clinical manifestations of plague depend on the route of exposure. After an incubation period of 2-7 days, there is high fever and painful lymphadenopathy, commonly with greatly enlarged, tender nodes (buboes) in the neck, groin, or axillae.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Bartonella bacilliformis is the causative agent of Carrion's disease. This disease is characterized by two phases—Oroya fever and verruga peruana. Oroya fever presents with severe hemolytic anemia and fever. Verruga peruana involves the development of skin nodules.",
      "B": "Legionella pneumophila is associated with Legionnaires' disease, a severe form of pneumonia. It does not typically present with painful lymphadenopathy. Symptoms include high fever, cough, and pneumonia, which can progress to respiratory failure.",
      "C": "Clinical manifestations of plague depend on the route of exposure. After an incubation period of 2-7 days, there is high fever and painful lymphadenopathy, commonly with greatly enlarged, tender nodes (buboes) in the neck, groin, or axillae.",
      "D": "Pasteurella multocida is associated with infections from animal bites or scratches. It can cause cellulitis, abscess formation, and in severe cases, systemic manifestations. However, it is not typically associated with the medieval epidemic described."
    },
