      "A": "Bubonic plague is the most common form of plague. This occurs when an infected flea bites a person or when materials contaminated with Y. pestis enter through a break in a person’s skin.",
      "B": "While rats can harbor Yersinia pestis, the primary mode of transmission to humans is through flea bites, not direct rat bites. Rats themselves are not responsible for transmitting the disease directly to humans in the majority of cases.",
      "C": "Inhalation is more relevant to the pneumonic form of plague, not the bubonic form. The bubonic form is primarily transmitted through the bite of an infected flea.",
      "D": "This is not a typical mode of transmission for bubonic plague. The bacterium is mainly transmitted through the bite of an infected flea, leading to the development of bubonic plague."
