    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The virulent yersiniae produce V and W antigens, which are encoded by genes on a plasmid of approximately 70kb. This is essential for virulence; the V and W antigens yield the requirement for calcium for growth.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The virulent yersiniae produce V and W antigens, which are encoded by genes on a plasmid of approximately 70kb. This is essential for virulence; the V and W antigens yield the requirement for calcium for growth.",
      "B": "While Yersinia pestis does produce various proteases, they may not be considered essential for virulence. Other factors, such as the V and W antigens and the type III secretion system, play more significant roles in the pathogenicity of Yersinia pestis.",
      "C": "Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) is a component of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria, including Yersinia pestis. While LPS contributes to the virulence of the organism, it is not considered the sole essential factor. Other factors, such as the V and W antigens, are also crucial for the virulence of Yersinia pestis.",
      "D": "Yersinia pestis does produce hemolysin, but its role in virulence is not as central as some other factors. The V and W antigens, along with other factors like the type III secretion system, are more critical for the pathogenicity of Yersinia pestis."
    },
