    "id": "notion-microbiology-0095",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 95,
    "stem": "Situation 27- A number of adult patient in a certain community hospital manifested with similar clinical symptoms. The illness starts with malaise, fever, headache, sore throat and non-productive cough. Later, cough became productive with sputum, sometimes blood-steaked. Blood examination showed mild leukocytosis While chest radiographs showed pulmonary consolidation, the patients were up and about, and appeared physically well. Treatment with penicillin, cephalosporin, and vancomycin failed but treatment with tetracycline and erythromycin were effective. The usual bacteriologic method failed to isolate the organism. Which of the following is the MOST likely bacterial agent?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Haemophilus influenzae"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Chlamydia pneumoniae"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Mycoplasma pneumonia"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Clinical findings: Incubation period varies from 1-3wks. Onset is usually insidious, with malaise, fever, headache, sore throat, and cough. Initially, the cough is nonproductive, but it is occasionally paroxysmal. Later, there may be blood steaked sputum and chest pain. Early in the course, the patient appears only moderately ill, and physical signs of pulmonary consolidation are often negligible compared with the striking consolidation seen on radiograph.\nTreatment: Tetracyclines, macrolides, fluoroquinolones",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "While Haemophilus influenzae can cause respiratory infections, it is less likely in this scenario, especially given the failure of treatment with penicillin, cephalosporin, and vancomycin.",
      "B": "Chlamydia pneumoniae is a possible cause of atypical pneumonia, but the failure of traditional bacteriologic methods to isolate the organism and the effective response to tetracycline and erythromycin make Mycoplasma pneumonia a more likely candidate.",
      "C": "Clinical findings: Incubation period varies from 1-3wks. Onset is usually insidious, with malaise, fever, headache, sore throat, and cough. Initially, the cough is nonproductive, but it is occasionally paroxysmal. Later, there may be blood steaked sputum and chest pain. Early in the course, the patient appears only moderately ill, and physical signs of pulmonary consolidation are often negligible compared with the striking consolidation seen on radiograph.\nTreatment: Tetracyclines, macrolides, fluoroquinolones",
      "D": "Streptococcus pneumoniae is a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia, but the failure of treatment with penicillin, cephalosporin, and vancomycin makes it less likely in this case."
    },
