    "id": "notion-microbiology-0097",
    "subject": "microbiology",
    "originalNumber": 97,
    "stem": "How is the diagnosis usually made in this illness?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Clinical recognition"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Gram-stain"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Serology"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Nucleic acid amplification test"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The diagnosis of M. pneumoniae pneumonia is primarily based on clinical recognition of the characteristic syndrome.\nClinical Features: Clinical manifestations include an insidious onset with malaise, fever, headache, sore throat, and cough. The cough may become productive with blood-streaked sputum, and chest radiographs may show pulmonary consolidation. Patients often appear moderately ill despite radiographic findings. The term “Walking pneumonia” applies to Mycoplasma infections.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "The diagnosis of M. pneumoniae pneumonia is primarily based on clinical recognition of the characteristic syndrome.\nClinical Features: Clinical manifestations include an insidious onset with malaise, fever, headache, sore throat, and cough. The cough may become productive with blood-streaked sputum, and chest radiographs may show pulmonary consolidation. Patients often appear moderately ill despite radiographic findings. The term “Walking pneumonia” applies to Mycoplasma infections.",
      "B": "Gram-staining is of limited use in diagnosing Mycoplasma pneumoniae infections because these bacteria lack a cell wall. Traditional Gram-staining, which relies on the presence of a cell wall, may not effectively visualize Mycoplasma organisms.",
      "C": "Serological tests, such as the measurement of antibodies (IgM and IgG), can aid in the diagnosis of Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection. An increase in specific antibodies over time may suggest recent infection. However, they can be expensive an impractical in most settings.",
      "D": "NAAT, particularly polymerase chain reaction (PCR)-based tests, can detect the presence of Mycoplasma pneumoniae DNA in clinical samples.\nPCR tests offer high sensitivity and specificity and can be useful in confirming the presence of the organism.\nHowever, they can be expensive an impractical in most settings."
    },
